  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will compute and
    interpret the correlation coefficient for a bivariate, numerical data set.</para>
<para>When
    you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list>
  <item>Describe
      the strength of a relationship between two variables.</item>
  <item>
    <para>Use
        technology to find the correlation coefficient and explain what the value tells you about a linear model in everyday language.</para>
  </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <b>activities</b> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<title>Lesson Activities</title>
<para>Here are the instructional
  activities for the lesson:</para>
<list>
  <item>3.5.0: Lesson Overview</item>
  <item>3.5.1: Using Bivariate Data in
    Context</item>
  <item>3.5.2: Interpreting the Strength of
    Linear Relationships
    <list>
      <item>3.5.2: Self Check</item>
      <item>3.5.2: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>3.5.3: Describing Relationships between Two
    Variables
    <list>
      <item>3.5.3: Self Check</item>
      <item>3.5.3: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>3.5.4: Interpreting the Correlation Coefficient</item>
</list>
<para>After
    that, you’ll <b>practice and review</b>.</para>
<list>
  <item>3.5.5: Practice</item>
  <item>3.5.6: Lesson Summary</item>
</list>
</section>
